# Remote Control API — Capabilities

Nullcast (`app-flyleaf/`) exposes a **local HTTP control surface** so other applications on
the machine — voice agents, a control panel, automation scripts — can drive playback, tell
the player what to play, and read or subscribe to the current playback state.

This page is a **capability overview**. The exhaustive request/response reference (bodies,
error envelopes, configuration, SSE frame format) lives in
[VIDEO-PLAYER-API.md](../../VIDEO-PLAYER-API.md); implementation is HAVOC **F-654**
(`Services/RemoteControlService.cs`, `MainWindow.Api.cs`).

> [!NOTE]
> The API only exists while the player app is running, and is enabled by default. It binds
> loopback-only unless LAN mode is explicitly turned on. All times are **milliseconds**; all
> timestamps are **Unix epoch milliseconds**.

## At a glance

| | |
| --- | --- |
| Transport | Plain HTTP (JSON) on `localhost`, plus a Server-Sent-Events stream for live state |
| Base URL (default) | `http://127.0.0.1:47893/api/v1` |
| Auth (default) | none on loopback; `Authorization: Bearer <token>` required in LAN mode |
| Protocol | `v1` |

## What it can do

The API groups into four capability areas.

### 1. Transport control

Drive the currently loaded media, mirroring the on-screen transport bar. Every command
returns the resulting [state document](#4-read--subscribe-to-state) so the caller learns the
effect in one round-trip.

| Capability | Endpoint | Notes |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Resume | `POST /play` *(no body)* | Resume if paused. |
| Pause | `POST /pause` | |
| Toggle play/pause | `POST /playpause` | |
| Stop | `POST /stop` | Stops and clears now-playing. |
| Next | `POST /next` | Skip to the next queue entry / TV episode. |
| Previous | `POST /previous` | Previous entry / episode; restarts the current item first when >3s in, matching the transport button. |
| Seek | `POST /seek` | Absolute (`positionMs`) or relative (`deltaMs`), clamped to duration. |
| Volume / mute | `POST /volume` | Set (`level` 0–100), adjust (`delta`), and/or `mute`. |

> [!NOTE]
> **Next/Previous step the existing queue.** They respect the same flat-vs-Plex queue split
> as the UI buttons, so they walk playlist/podcast/YouTube-Music tracks or Plex TV episodes
> (across seasons) depending on what's playing. They do not build or reorder a queue.

### 2. Play by reference

Start playback of a specific item by handing the player an identifier it can resolve. An
optional `startPositionMs` sets the resume point.

| Source `type` | Required field | Resolved via |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `youtube` | `url` (+ optional `quality`) | yt-dlp |
| `url` | `url` | Direct FFmpeg open (falls back to yt-dlp for page URLs) |
| `file` | `path` | Direct FFmpeg open |
| `plex` | `ratingKey` | Plex Direct Play (server must be configured in-app) |
| `bookmark` | `muid` | Sygnal Playlist bookmark (uses saved resume unless overridden) |

Resolution runs **asynchronously**: a `POST /play` with a `source` returns quickly and the
state transitions `loading` → `playing`. Subscribe to `/events` (or poll `/state`) to observe
it. The API reuses the same play paths as the UI — no parallel playback logic.

### 3. Read & subscribe to state

| Capability | Endpoint | Returns |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Discovery / health | `GET /ping` | `{app, version, protocol, state}` — unauthenticated |
| Current state | `GET /state` | The canonical state document |
| Live state stream | `GET /events` | `text/event-stream`; a `state` frame on every meaningful change |

The **state document** is returned by every command and by `GET /state`, so reads and writes
share one shape:

| Field | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| `state` | `idle` \| `loading` \| `playing` \| `paused` \| `stopped` \| `ended` |
| `item` | now-playing (`type`, `title`, `sourceUrl`, `muid`, `ratingKey`); `null` when idle |
| `positionMs` / `durationMs` | Playback position and length |
| `volume` / `muted` | `0–100` and mute flag |
| `speed` / `fullscreen` / `workspaceId` | Playback rate, fullscreen flag, active workspace |
| `app` / `ts` | App/protocol info and the snapshot's epoch-ms timestamp |

Over `/events`, transport, now-playing, and volume/mute changes are pushed immediately;
position ticks are throttled to about **1 per second**. A Plex `sourceUrl` is always the
stable `plex://<ratingKey>` form — the server token is never exposed.

### 4. Security & configuration

| Capability | Behaviour |
| --- | --- |
| Safe by default | Loopback bind, no token, no CORS headers unless configured. |
| LAN access | Opt-in (`bind: "lan"`); always token-guarded; a failed LAN bind degrades to loopback rather than failing open. |
| Token auth | `Authorization: Bearer <token>` on every request except `GET /ping`; token stored DPAPI-encrypted at rest. |
| Additive | When disabled, the listener never starts and playback behaves exactly as before. |

Configuration lives in `%AppData%\VideoPlayer\api.json`; changes take effect on app restart.
See [VIDEO-PLAYER-API.md](../../VIDEO-PLAYER-API.md) §6 for the full config schema and the LAN
URL-ACL steps.

## What it deliberately can't do

These are out of scope for the current API (candidates for future work):

- **Internet / cross-NAT control.** A cloud-relayed command path (via the Sygnal Playlist
  Cloudflare service, reusing the existing OAuth identity) is the intended future route.
- **Queue / playlist management** — enqueue, reorder, or jump to an arbitrary queue position.
  Stepping the *existing* queue works (`/next`, `/previous`); building or rearranging it does not.
- **Media search** over the API. Callers pass identifiers they already have.
- **Per-caller identity** beyond a single shared bearer token.

## Quick reference

```sh
# Health check (no auth)
curl http://127.0.0.1:47893/api/v1/ping

# Transport
curl -X POST http://127.0.0.1:47893/api/v1/playpause
curl -X POST http://127.0.0.1:47893/api/v1/next
curl -X POST http://127.0.0.1:47893/api/v1/previous
curl -X POST http://127.0.0.1:47893/api/v1/seek   -H 'Content-Type: application/json' -d '{"deltaMs":-15000}'
curl -X POST http://127.0.0.1:47893/api/v1/volume -H 'Content-Type: application/json' -d '{"level":50}'

# Play a specific item
curl -X POST http://127.0.0.1:47893/api/v1/play -H 'Content-Type: application/json' -d '{
  "source": { "type": "plex", "ratingKey": "12345" }
}'

# Live state
curl -N http://127.0.0.1:47893/api/v1/events
```
